From ce3e58abf1985334111b8e57fc34b82dcdd351d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 16:25:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 26/28] console: implement krun_set_console_output outside
 aws-nitro

The symbol was gated on `aws-nitro`, an unrelated backend, with a FIXME
saying as much. containerd's nerdbox resolves its whole libkrun binding
table eagerly at LoadLibrary time, so the one missing export failed the
load of the entire DLL: 18 of the 19 symbols it binds resolved, and
krun_set_console_output returned GetLastError=127.

Gate it on `not(aws-nitro)` instead, and give it an implementation on the
regular VMM path: attach a virtio-console with a single console port
whose output goes to the caller's sink. The port carries terminal
properties (fixed 0x0) so the guest marks it VIRTIO_CONSOLE_CONSOLE_PORT
and exposes it as hvcN, which is what nerdbox's `console=hvc0` command
line needs. It deliberately does not reuse autoconfigure_console_ports(),
which routes console output to the VMM log whenever the output is not a
tty -- precisely what a caller passing an explicit path does not want.
The aws-nitro build keeps its old behaviour of recording the path.

Windows is not symmetric with Unix here, and cannot be. On Unix the
consumer owns the path: nerdbox creates the FIFO and opens its read end
before calling in, so libkrun only opens the write end (O_NONBLOCK, so
"no reader" fails with ENXIO rather than hanging the caller). On Windows
nerdbox passes a `\\.\pipe\krun-console-<pid>-<n>` name and then *dials*
it with winio.DialPipe(), so libkrun must serve the pipe. It is created
in krun_set_console_output() so the name exists before the consumer's
first dial, duplex because DialPipe() asks for GENERIC_READ|GENERIC_WRITE
and an outbound-only pipe would refuse it, and overlapped so that the
ConnectNamedPipe() that has to precede the first write can be bounded.

That connect happens on the port's tx thread, not in the configuration
call, which could not block there without deadlocking the consumer that
only starts dialing once it returns. If it times out, or if the client
later disappears, the sink falls back to the VMM log after one
error-level message naming the pipe: discarding the output silently, or
letting the tx thread stop, would surface as an unexplained guest hang
once the console virtqueue filled.

